  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong cold front moves through this evening.\n\nStrong cold front moving through southern Maryland as of 0030Z\nOct 17. Some rain developing on the backside with gradient winds\npicking up. Nothing hazardous in terms of rain or wind aside\nfrom over the waters. Forecast remains on track tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and/or freezing temperatures are possible\nthis weekend.\n\nGiven very favorable setup for radiational cooling, expanded\nFreeze Watch to central MD and western Loudoun where\nprobabilities for near 32F exist. Previous discussion follows...\n\nStrong cold advection will ensue within northwesterly flow \nbehind the cold front tonight through the day tomorrow. Upslope\nshowers in the Alleghenies may even end as a few flakes of snow\nlate tonight, but no accumulation is expected. Temperatures are\nforecast to drop into the 40s by daybreak tomorrow (mid- upper \n30s mountains). A few showers could linger from Central Virginia\nto Southern Maryland tomorrow morning, but gradual clearing is \nexpected tomorrow afternoon as the upper trough axis progresses \nto our east and large scale subsidence starts to increase. \nTemperatures will be much cooler tomorrow, with highs in the 50s\n(40s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure will gradually build in from the west tomorrow\nnight as a nor'easter simultaneously starts to develop off the \nCarolina coastline. The main question tomorrow night is which \nlocations will decouple. The setup for radiational cooling will \nbe nearly ideal in locations that go calm, with clear skies \nexpected and dewpoints in the 20s. Temperatures will likely \nplummet into the 20s and lower 30s where winds go calm. \nConfidence is currently the highest for winds going calm to the \nwest of the Blue Ridge, with greater uncertainty with respect to\nthe winds further east as the nor'easter develops offshore. A \nFreeze Watch has been issued to the west of the Blue Ridge, \nwhere confidence is highest for sub-freezing temperatures. Some \nfrost or sub-freezing temperatures can't be ruled out further \neast, either. Forecast confidence decreases further moving into\nSunday. Forecast models show considerable spread with respect \nto the location of the aforementioned nor'easter during that \ntime period. Some solutions keep the system offshore, while \nothers have it retrograding further westward, which could lead \nto impacts locally.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Nor'easter to develop off the Carolina coast late\nin the weekend lingers much of next week.\n\nPart of the upper trough that swings through tonight into \nSaturday will cutoff along the front stalling to the south later\nthis weekend. This result in the development of a nor'easter \noff the Carolina coast. This system then meanders offshore/near \nthe coast, but guidance varies widely on its placement and \nstrength. Since it will be cut off from the main steering flow, \nit could persist much of next week. Northeast breezes and \noccasional rain chances are possible, with the highest chances \nfurther east.",
